Date: Tue, 2 Mar 93 13:59:09 GMT
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: POLI: Free speech?

Alexander Chislenko asks:
>Where's that profound difference between speech and other ways of passing
>information?

[various examples omitted]

>   Can anybody explain me the sacred value of speech / info and answer the
>above questions?

Timothy May gave a full answer to this.  Just one point of his I'd like to
expand on:
>What it really means is that various levels of government are not
>to make laws about what is and what is not acceptable speech, writing, or
>thought. (There are some obvious exceptions, like obscenity, espionage,
>libel, etc. Some of us are very worried that more and more things are being
>added to this category.)

It is obvious that such exceptions are made, not obvious to me that there
is justification for making any such exceptions at all.

Rather than dilute my point by putting forward my own list of forms of
expression I might be persuaded to ban, let me make the simple contention:
there should be *no* laws abridging freedom of speech.

-- Libel can be dealt with as a tort.

-- Many activites can constitute espionage and can be punished as such,
there is nothing special about speech as a particular means of espionage.

-- Trade secrets are the responsibility of those who own them to keep
secret.  Encryption, physical security, and explicit contractual provisions
with their partners and employees are the ways to protect them.

-- Obscenity, blasphemy, and indecent exposure are the inventions of fools
and knaves.  (Was it Heinlein who picked out these three as the most
ludicrous prohibitions ever?)

-- The examples that Alexander Chislenko gave all fall under the principle
that freedom of expression does not carry with it the right to make anyone
listen or to use their resources to express oneself.

-- Scrap the notions of copyright and patenting as well. The legal concept
of intellectual property vanishes with them, other than the right to pursue
those who pass off your work as theirs or theirs as yours (see libel,
above).

I used the dread word "should" above, so I had better say that by saying
there "should" be no such laws I mean only that I would find it an
improvement if all such laws were scrapped, and suggest that other
extropians might also find it so.  Is there anyone here to speak up in
favour of any such laws, whether within current statist regimes or under
PPL?

