Date: Tue, 2 Mar 93 10:20:38 -0800
From: cappello%cs@hub.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)
Subject: POLI: Free speech?

Here is a response that lacks legal expertise.  Upon the scrutiny of  
experts, it may prove defective. (Indeed, I may not like it  
tomorrow.)

Free speech (i.e., uncensored) is to be distinguished from other  
activities between consenting adults that, in this country, despite  
their consensual nature, have been prohibited by the state.  I  
believe that libertarians do not make this distinction:  Libertarians  
believe that behavior between consenting adults should not be  
prohibited by law.  Thus, libertarians take a view that answers some  
of your objections.  


One of the adults from whom consent must be obtained is the owner of  
the property where the acts are performed---speech or otherwise.  For  
example, the owner of the wedding premises may not allow users to  
masturbate publicly on his premises (a business decision).

>My son doesn't want to hear the contents of the movie before  
>watching it; it SPOILS the fun. Am I free to tell him?

For the purposes of this discussion, he is an adult.  That is, if you  
tell him over his protest (e.g., forcibly prevent him from covering  
his ears with his hands), then you are initiating coercion.  One  
might describe it as a forced penetration of his ears by your  
speech--- an "auditory rape."  If not illegal, it is certainly  
impolite, by libertarian standards. 


>I would agree that harmless things should be free, regardless of  
>their nature, and harmful things may be reasonably restricted, also  
>regardless.

Harm is more a matter of taste than consent.  Insisting that the act  
be harmless thus is more problematical than insisting that it be  
consensual.  The latter approach does not admit, unlike the former,  
invasions of privacy by uninvolved parties.  An act can be construed  
as harmful by one not involved, but nonetheless be consensual.  Sex,  
drugs, and rock and roll provide ample examples.  If one freely  
ingests some nutrient, then consent clearly is implicit, but doing so  
may be judged by others as harmful.

>Can anybody explain me the sacred value of speech / info and answer  
>the above questions?

Intersect the set of acts between consenting adults with the set of  
acts of speech. For most Americans, such acts should be free (i.e.,  
they should not be prohibited by the state). For most Americans, this  
freedom is sacred, and unique to speech.  For libertarians, this  
freedom is sacred but not unique to speech.  We apply it to the  
entire set of acts between consenting adults, including, for example,  
capitalistic acts.

-Pete
