Date: Tue, 2 Mar 93 10:35:04 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: BOMB: Who could have done this?

Garrett Goebel writes:

>Anyone can make a nuclear bomb.  The hard part is getting the high grade
>uranium or plutonium.  All you have to do is incase the uranium/plutonium
>and set up a small explosive charge that will force all the radioactive
>material close together... like a grenade that explodes inward.

Not "anyone." I was a physicist and I certainly don't think I could build
even a crude bomb with a reasonable chance of it working. I read the
"Smythe Report" in high school, "The Progressive" article on basement
nukes, Irwin Strauss's overly-hyped book on how to build a nuke (I forget
the exact title, but Loompanics sells it), and so on.

I used to think I could build a crude one by dropping one subcritical U-235
mass down a massive pipe from 2-3 floors up (inertial driver, with a few
hundred pounds of concrete or steel behind the falling mass) and into the
other subcritical piece., sort of like the original "Little Boy" uranium
bomb. Stuff I've learned more recently suggests this wouldn't work
(nonuniformity of the explosion). In any case, having no test facilities
would be a severe crimp.

Tom Clancy's "Sum of All Fears" (I think it was) was more plausible--a
fairly sophisticated lab group gets ahold of a partly-functional nuke and
makes necessary modifications (SPOILER WARNING: despite this expertise, it
still "fizzles.").

I suspect some nukes, tactical or demolition, are "lost" from retreating
Soviet forces in the former USSR. No proof of this, just a guess. There are
recurring reports (rumors, really) that some of these are already being
offered for sale on the black market.

>The Iraqies (sp?) are offering $100,000 a year to soviet scientists with
>useful knowledge... who on their own would pull in less than $1,000 a year.

True.

>Luckily, the current problem is in delivery systems... so while everyone 
>should have nukes within 10 years... they won't be able to stick their
>bombs in ballistic missiles and deliver them over long ranges.  Instead,
>they will have to smuggle the radioactive materials in to the country
>and leave a car bomb somewhere... we are going to see nuclear warfare in
>regional conflicts within the next 10-20 years, and probably and increase
>in increasingly dangerous car bombings.

Most ranges they need are not ballistic...several hundred miles should do
for most regional conflicts. On the other hand, some groups will not want a
trail pointing back to them, and so will use more surreptitious delivery
systems, as Garrett notes.

>On the other hand... long range delivery systems aren't that far off for
>many developing countries either.  If you hear countries calling for the
>need to develop their own satelite launching capabilities, you are listening
>to a countries that is developing ballistic missile capability.  It is 
>cheaper and more effective for countries to outsource satelite launching
>needs than to develop their own capabilities.

Some people in the private space launch business (such as it is) told me
that they stayed well clear of advertising their products as delivery
systems. One noted space enthusiast (initials: GH) apparently stood up a
conference and openly talked about "Your own private delivery system"--this
caused a flap.

Some types of private research are banned in the U.S. For example, chemical
and biological warfare research is strictly controlled (private citizens
and corporations may not work in this area, unless they have the
appropriate government contracts, clearances, and controls). Ditto, of
course, for nuclear weapons research. I suspect there are limits on rocket
research, too.

And cryptography work may soon be similarly limited.

-Tim May
